ial  okay great another question that came  through about  construct size so is there a limit that  can be  electroporated effectively into cells  regarding the construct size  okay is this question in relation to car  tcr  particularly or just in general  so if it is in relation to car tcr the  case study  that i showed with the transposon car  that those constructs are 6 kb  roughly each we do get efficient  transduction  transfection efficiencies to 8 or 9 kb  of car  dna but then for protein production and  other applications we have gone  we have electroporated much larger uh  sizes  and for viral vector production  sometimes the size is also very large  so yeah all right that's helpful  another question has come through about  cytokine release syndrome and its effect  on  car t cell therapy can you speak to the  cytokine release  syndrome effect rama yes so  cytokine release syndrome is a common  side effect that's found in car  therapies it's to a lesser extent in tcr  therapies but basically  when a car therapy is really effective  you get you know massive uh in the  course of tumor elimination and  recruitment of  all the immune cells you get a release  of a number of cytokines il-6  particularly  that causes high fevers and patients and  is commonly called as the cytokine  release syndrome  and um that that's like a commonly  seen side effect and most  virally induced car therapies  okay effective good yeah okay  next question does maxite's  electroporation process  affect the cell surface receptor  expression on nk cells  okay so that's a good question so the  maxsite electroporation  process is a fairly inert process  so according to the published data the  people that have used this  instrument for not just for uh nk  car expression but for other  uh you know adcc related studies done in  nk cells they basically found that there  was no effect  after electroporation on any of the  cell surface receptor expressions like  nkg2d  or cd16 that was evaluated by facts  analysis  post electroporation  okay next questions regarding viability  so  a customer typed in what is the  viability of the cells  12 and 24 hours post ep  so so this is in relation to  mrna transfections or  dna transfections it does  yeah okay yeah so so mrna transfections  is well as we all know you know in  immune cells mrna transfection does not  affect viability at all  and it's usually well in the 90s  either six hours post transfection or 24  hours  uh post transfection uh dna  uh you know immune cells are pretty  resistant to taking up dna  as such so dna does affect  viability but it also depends on the  concentration  of dna used so there's a linear  correlation between the amount of dna  and viability so the large as you  increase the amount of dna this dna  related toxicity  so let's say for example 100 micrograms  per mill of dna you would not see that  which is what we see  in dr yagyu's work which had over  86 percent uh viability post  dna transfection and that was two dnas  the transposon and transpose that was  uh that were co-transfected i don't know  okay  it does thank you um next question can  the maxite flow electroporation  technology  be integrated with commonly used culture  systems to create a fully closed process  yes so that is a really good question  and the answer is yes max cite  the any of those instruments the  atx well the atx is usually a research  grade instrument so the flow  electroporation technologies that  basically require higher uh cell  manipulations  can be sterile welded  into with other devices like the direct  devices that's commonly used for growing  t  cells or wave bioreactors which is done  downstream of the electroporation  process  or it even is bio welded quite well with  the  miltoni system so yes the answer is it  can be sterile welded  and can fit into the workflow of a  customer pretty easily  okay next question um is about the  electroporation parameter so  customer typed in can these  elect